rep("(4) A Difference-in-Differences on Eurostat NACE A64 data estimates with TWFE a divergence of the wage share in privatized/liberalized sectors (energy, telecommunications, transport) relative to controls of −16.54 percentage points (wild cluster bootstrap p=0.022), but the modern staggered estimator of Callaway-Sant'Anna on the same data yields an ATT of opposite sign (+2.3 percentage points, 95% CI [+0.3; +4.3]) with non-flat pre-trends: at the NACE A64 branch level the distributive effect of privatizations is not identified, and the result is downgraded to descriptive evidence. The Modiano-Onado and Torrini thesis is reformulated as non-reinvested quasi-rents, whose aggregate signature is in the Kalecki decomposition.",
    "(4) On Eurostat national accounts by branch, 1995-2024, the sectors privatized in the 1990s show a rising profit share and a falling share of reinvested surplus (energy: profit share from 60 to 78 per cent, reinvested surplus from 169 to 73 per cent, real value added declining; finance: from 40 to 57 and from 25 to 15), while manufacturing, exposed to competition, does the opposite (profit share from 49 to 45, reinvested surplus from 39 to 55). Torrini's (2005) expectation of an expansion of the privatized sectors did not materialize; the Modiano-Onado (2023) thesis — positional rents not reinvested, grafted onto wages already compressed by 1992-93 — is confirmed. A difference-in-differences on the wage share of privatized branches, attempted in earlier versions, was abandoned: not identifiable and unable to distinguish compressed wages from rents.")
